  "query_preview": "..You may have been woken up before your alarm went off\nthis morning as scattered showers and storms rolled through the \nTucson Metro from the northwest to southeast. That, or maybe it was \nthe uncontainable excitement for the first day of Monsoon 2026 \nstarting off with the nostalgic sound of thunder and rain that we \nhave so dearly missed. Shower and thunderstorm activity this \nafternoon will probably be suppressed in the wake of the surprise \nshower and storm activity across eastern Pima, Pinal, and western \nGraham/Cochise counties due to the atmosphere being worked over and \nblocked from clouds through the late morning hours. Precipitable \nwater east of a Tucson/Nogales line measured between 0.7 to 1.2 \ninches. To the west of Tucson, skies were mostly clear allowing for \nadequate insolation this afternoon coinciding with precipitable \nwater around 1.2 to 1.4 inches. Surface dewpoints were in the 50s to \nlow 60s across the area.\n\nWith sufficient moisture in place, all we need is some time for the \natmosphere to destabilize and for a favorable lifting mechanism. The \nlifting mechanism will come from terrain as well as a deformation \nzone stretching from southwest Pinal County to western Santa Cruz \nCounty. Taking a peek at the high resolution convective allowing \nmodels in the HREF, we see general agreement of storms firing along \nthe aforementioned deformation zone in central/western Pima county \nand southwest Pinal county around 1 PM MST supporting my theory. \nOutflows from these storms may then result in a chain reaction of \nshower and thunderstorm activity (20-30%) mainly west of the I-10 \nand I-19 corridors late this afternoon. Shower and storm activity \ncould linger into the evening hours (10-20%) as they become outflow \ndominant. While there is a small chance (10-20%) of showers and \nstorms reaching the Tucson Metro, I am not as confident in this \nscenario due to the recent shower and storm activity.\n\nThe main hazards the environment supports today are locally heavy \nrain and strong gusty outflow winds. There is some potential for \nblowing dust from Santa Cruz County north into central Pima County \nand southwest Pinal County. The HREF depicts a small area where \nthere is a 10 percent 40-km neighborhood probability of winds in \nexcess of 50 kts. Rain amounts with the strongest storms will be \nlocally be 1 to 1.5 inches.\n\nLooking at Tuesday, the deepest moisture slip south. Precipitable \nwater will decrease slightly with only isolated to scattered \ncoverage of storms (20 to 30 percent chance) mainly along the Int'l \nborder eastward with a slight (10 percent) chance elsewhere. The \nbreak in activity may not last very long as divergence aloft \nincreases out ahead of a weak upper low that will move east out of \nCalifornia Wednesday. The upper low may provide a bit more favorable \nlift resulting in an uptick in coverage of shower and thunderstorm \nactivity by Wednesday afternoon into Wednesday evening from Tucson \nsouth and eastward. There continues to be uncertainty in the upper \nlevel pattern in the Thursday/Friday timeframe, but still enough \nmoisture for isolated to scattered storms mainly south and east of \nTucson, especially if thunderstorm moist air can be recycled. \nHowever, dry southwesterly flow should begin to shunt moisture away \nfrom southeast Arizona by the weekend into early next week with \nprecipitation chances dropping off by the weekend.\n\nTemperatures will be near normal through the forecast period with \nbreezy west to northwest winds each afternoon. Winds will especially \nbe gusty Tuesday through Thursday in the Safford area where gusts of \n30+ mph are expected.",
